    <string name="calib_section_howto">How it works</string>
    <string name="calib_howto">This is a home check, not a laboratory calibration: a real one is done against a reference black body. The camera is compared with things whose temperature is known without it: melting ice, boiling water or a contact thermometer.\n\nA measurement takes the mean inside the frame in the middle of the image over three seconds, at ε 0.96 (water) and without the current correction. One reference shifts all readings. Two references at least 30 °C apart, such as ice and boiling water, also give a slope.\n\nA correction that would move readings by more than 10 °C is refused: a difference that large almost always means a failed measurement. The camera constants stay as they are, and the correction can be reset at any time.</string>
